---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Field from '../components/Field.astro';
import Hale from '../components/Hale.astro';
const reject = `// you write:
locus Reader {
    bus { publish Frame; }
}
// hale check:
//   error: publish references unknown topic 'Frame'
//   (no 'topic Frame' declaration in this file)`;
---
<Base title="The toolchain" description="One binary: build, run, check, verify, test, bench, fmt, doc, fetch and fleet, with the language server and the MCP server compiled in.">
  <Field />
  <section class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>One binary: build, run, check, verify, test, bench, fmt, doc, fetch and fleet.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything ships in <code>hale</code>, with a language server and an
        MCP server compiled into the same binary. The same structured
        diagnostic reaches an editor, a CI job and a coding assistant.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The compiler answers quickly and precisely</h2>
      <p>
        <code>hale check</code> returns structured, location-precise
        diagnostics, fast enough to run on every keystroke and specific enough
        to act on without reading around them.
      </p>
      <Hale code={reject} title="hale-check" kind="diagnostic" />
      <p class="muted">
        Unknown fields, unknown topics, and methods that return a managed
        entity are hard errors. Those bug classes stop at build time.
      </p>

      <h2>In the editor</h2>
      <p>
        <code>hale lsp</code> speaks the Language Server Protocol: diagnostics
        as you type, hover, go to definition and completion in any editor that
        speaks it. <code>hale fmt</code> keeps one canonical style. <code>hale doc</code> renders API reference from <code>///</code> comments, and <code>--json</code> gives the same for machines.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mcp">For a coding assistant</h2>
      <p>
        <code>hale mcp</code> exposes the toolchain over the Model Context
        Protocol: <code>hale_check</code>, <code>hale_build</code>, <code>hale_run</code>, <code>hale_fmt</code>, <code>hale_fetch</code> and <code>hale_docs_search</code>, for any MCP host, including ones
        without a shell.
      </p>
      <div class="install" role="group" aria-label="Add the Hale MCP server">
        <span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
        <span class="cmd">claude mcp add hale -- hale mcp</span>
      </div>

      <h2 id="context-pack">The documentation, for machines</h2>
      <p>
        The documentation ships in forms built to be read programmatically: <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> is the index and <a href="/llms-full.txt" download>llms-full.txt</a> is the full specification
        corpus. Two bundled context packs put the essentials, the spec and the
        real-world gotchas in one file. The slim pack is about 14k tokens.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/hale-context-slim.txt" download>Context pack, slim</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/hale-context.txt" download>Full pack</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/llms-full.txt">llms-full.txt</a>
      </div>

      <h2>Rules files</h2>
      <p>
        Rules files distilled from the style guide, the two-channel
        discipline, the pattern catalog and the gotchas, in the formats coding
        tools pick up on their own.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/agent/AGENTS.md" download>AGENTS.md</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/agent/CLAUDE.md" download>CLAUDE.md</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/agent/.cursorrules" download>.cursorrules</a>
      </div>

      <h2>Every subcommand</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>build · run</b><p>One static binary from a file or a project; run it in place.</p></li>
        <li><b>check · verify</b><p>The whole-project check, and the stricter gate that turns every advisory into a failure.</p></li>
        <li><b>test · bench</b><p>Tests and benchmarks from the same tree, with the same diagnostics.</p></li>
        <li><b>fmt · doc</b><p>One canonical style; reference rendered from <code>///</code> comments, as a page or as JSON.</p></li>
        <li><b>fetch</b><p>Vendor a pinned git dependency into the tree.</p></li>
        <li><b>fleet</b><p>Compose the topology artifacts of separately built binaries and check law across them.</p></li>
        <li><b>describe · call · watch · admin <span class="ev">main</span></b><p>Clients for a served API surface: read its description, call a member, follow a stream, open a dashboard. They need no code from the program.</p></li>
        <li><b>lsp · mcp</b><p>The language server and the MCP server, in the same binary. <code>hale mcp --app</code> serves a program's own API surface to an MCP host as tools.</p></li>
        <li><b>dna</b><p>The organization over a codebase. <a href="/dna">Its own page.</a></p></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
